@*
    About page. Scott's strongest honest card: 25 years, ex-agency, direct. Copy is
    placeholder and heavily his to write, especially the personal narrative. Every claim
    baked in here is BACKED (25 years .NET, agency background, direct, backend depth,
    Jambalam). Do NOT invent biography: employers, schools, hometown, dates. Those are
    marked as prompts for Scott to fill, not written for him.
*@

<section class="section--lede">
    <div class="container">
        <p class="topper">About</p>
        <h1 class="header">Scott Neidig</h1>
        @* No location line: the local-SEO framing came off the site with the rest of the
           contact and territory signals. *@
        <p class="text lede__text">
            Web and application developer. I've built .NET applications since 2005, and I work
            directly with businesses and organizations on the systems they run on.
        </p>

        @*
            Optional headshot. A face builds trust on an about page, but it's not required and
            it's Scott's to add. Drop the image in wwwroot and uncomment:
            <img class="about__photo" src="~/img/scott.jpg" alt="Scott Neidig" width="240" height="240" decoding="async" />
        *@
    </div>
</section>

<section class="section--alt">
    <div class="container">
        <p class="topper">Background</p>
        <h2 class="title">Twenty years in .NET</h2>

        @*
            Bio drafted from Scott's resume (Headless format). Every claim is backed by it:
            Havas Edge + Spinutech (agency .NET), certified nopCommerce developer, API/SQL work,
            version migrations, HIPAA security background, Jambalam + its Azure AI feature, and
            his stated use of AI tools in his workflow. Kept in his voice: plain, no em dashes,
            no hype. Scott's to sharpen. NOTE: says "since 2005" (resume start) not "25 years".
        *@
        <p class="text">
            I've been building web applications since 2005, most of that time in .NET and C#.
            A lot of it was agency work, building and maintaining sites and applications for
            clients: custom features, nopCommerce plugins, database work, version migrations,
            and production deployments. I'm a
            @* rel=noopener because target=_blank otherwise hands the new tab a reference back. *@
            <a href="https://www.nopcommerce.com/en/scott-neidig" target="_blank" rel="noopener">certified
            nopCommerce developer</a>, and I've spent years working directly with the people whose
            systems I'm in.
        </p>
        <p class="text">
            That work has spanned the full stack, not just the front end. I've built REST API
            integrations, designed and maintained SQL Server databases, moved stores across major
            nopCommerce versions without breaking their custom logic, and run deployments through
            CI/CD. Earlier I did HIPAA security work, checking healthcare applications for
            vulnerabilities, and I bring that same care to anything handling real data.
        </p>
        <p class="text">
            I've worked both inside agencies and directly with the businesses and organizations
            running the systems. I also build and run my own software, including Jambalam, a
            subscription platform on nopCommerce and ASP.NET Core with an AI-powered import
            feature built on Azure. I use AI tools in my daily work to move faster, and I check
            everything they produce.
        </p>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="section--alt">
    <div class="container">
        <p class="topper">Work</p>
        <h2 class="title">Past work</h2>
        <p class="text">
            Examples of what I've built, with the details of each project.
        </p>
        <p>
            <a asp-controller="Work" asp-action="Index">See past work</a>
        </p>
    </div>
</section>
